  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Extreme weather conditions such as drought and excessive rainfall weakens plants and increases chances of crop disease. The use of organic soil conditioners support plant health, increasing resilience to these events. 
&#x200d;
With support from the Market Development Facility (MDF), over 117 dalo-farming households across Fiji have started using Organic Plus as a soil conditioner on their farms and backyard gardens. The adoption of Organic Plus generated additional income exceeding FJD300,000 for participating households. 
&#x200d;
In Dawasamu, Tailevu on Fiji&apos;s main island of Viti Levu, farmer Raveena Lata attests to using Organic Plus.
&#x200d;
&#x201c;Two years back, my husband said the watermelon would be a big loss because of the heavy rain that month. But I didn&#x2019;t lose hope. My son sprayed liquid compost every 15 days and that gave me results. The watermelon was big. I sold it for FJD3 a kg and earned FJD4,500.&#x201d; 
&#x200d;
Research findings found watermelon farmers using Organic Plus earned on average FJD4,000 per household in additional income.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global supply chain disruptions and the rising cost of agricultural inputs create difficult conditions for high value agriculture in Fiji. MDF&#x2019;s work focusses on a vision of change where farmers have access to affordable, quality agriculture inputs, such as soil conditioners, seeds, small farm equipment and information. MDF partnered with agricultural input firms, such as Baywater Engineering, to introduce domestically produced organic fertiliser, a more affordable alternative to imported synthetic fertiliser. To support the uptake of small farm equipment and vertical farming practices, MDF partnered with Krishan&#x2019;s Farm Implements to roll out a machinery rental service that engaged farmers in different regions. And finally, MDF Pacific Regional partnered with Control Union to raise awareness of the value of different organic certifications in improving access to premium markets. Collectively, these partnerships have led to increased crop diversification and yields, including off-season crops.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Fiji, the Market Development Facility has worked with partners to improve their governance. This has enhanced decision-making and reduced risks promoting sustainable private sector led development in key industries.
&#x200d;
For example, with government partners, MDF has worked with the iTaukei Trust Fund Board to develop a tourism results measurement methodology and trained analysts in its use.  This has led TTFB to establish a new results measurement team and develop a framework that can be adopted by other indigenous institutions. With the Ministry of Trade, Co-operatives, SMEs and Communications, MDF supported the review of legislation in the mahogany industry, identifying more equitable payment models and better dispute resolution systems for community landowners. MDF&#x2019;s collaborative approach has helped the Ministry move from policy ambition to practical implementation, building internal confidence, systems and skills required to lead private sector development initiatives independently. Additionally, a mahogany sector investment prospectus with Investment Fiji is set to be released.
&#x200d;
Working with private sector partners, MDF worked with Baywater Organic and Post Fiji to distribute their product to over 50 locations including rural regions. The product is now more accessible to remote farming communities providing an accessible and sustainable agricultural input that will improve farm productivity.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The impacts of climate change, such as changing temperatures, extreme weather events and soil degradation, adversely affect crop growth, reducing farm yields and incomes. Drought and flood-resistant seeds can increase the resilience of farmers to extreme weather events exacerbated by climate change. Commercial results from the initial introduction of drought and flood resistant seeds into Fiji by Australia&#x2019;s Market Development Facility in partnership with East-West Seeds (EWS) are promising: farmers report that yields have improved and that they have been able to plant during the low season and capitalise on higher prices as a consequence. The program is now working with the private sector to expand the availability of high-quality seeds in Pacific Island countries and Timor-Leste, with 496 farming households benefitting in Fiji, and a company now distributing the seeds in Samoa.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
